To write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text, we can use a third-party library like pandoc or markdowncpp. 

Here's an example using pandoc:

```c++
#include <pandoc.h>
#include <iostream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    pandoc::Document doc = pandoc::read(markdown);
    pandoc::Document html = pandoc::write(pandoc::Format::HTML, doc);
    return html.to_string();
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "Hello, **world**!";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, we first read the markdown formatted text using pandoc::read() and then write it to html format using pandoc::write(). Finally, we return the html formatted text as a std::string.

Note that pandoc is a command-line tool, so we need to link against its library and include its header files in our C++ code. Also, pandoc has many options and features, so we can customize the output format and behavior to our needs.

